Debra I'm gonna start at the end there  is a credit the final credit we see  about this film inspired by Patrick  Perkins can you tell us who that person  is and how he inspired this film yeah I  was volunteering in a local public  school my mother had passed away I was  looking for something meaningful to do  and to give back and I met Patrick when  he was six years old he was a very  bright cheerful positive person already  showing signs of leadership when he was  six and as I was getting ready to go  back to work and I was walking out the  door I asked the assistant teacher what  Patrick's story was and she told me he  had been removed from his parents and he  was living in an orphanage called Holly  Grove which happens to be an orphanage  where Marilyn Monroe spent some time and  I had never encountered a foster youth  or the foster care system and I went  home sobbing and resolved to continue to  work with him and I never left it's been  25 years he just turned 31 and I view  him like him my son and he views me like  his mother Deborah the system has its  strengths and it has its weaknesses but  something that the system also has is a  respect for the privacy of the children  who are in the foster care system and  that as a filmmaker would present I  suspect some issues in terms of access  so how do you work within the system to  make sure that you have the trust of the  system and that then you can identify  the people that you want to follow  because I suspect it doesn't take a  couple of days maybe a couple of months  maybe more than that yeah we're very  lucky that the presiding judge of the  Los Angeles County System believed in a  transparent Court and he would always  try to invite people in and and they  wouldn't come primarily journalists but  other people as well so that was the  first step we CC 35 people on every  media request to the court to have  permission to film a child with an open  case and then we worked very closely  with children's Law Center the law firm  who represents all the children in the  system to make sure the  see rights of the children were honored  and respected but also that the children  understood what they were getting  themselves into particularly I mean  obviously for crystalline she's a  newborn child but in the case of Sydney  and danisha they took a vote and we met  mrs. beavers two years before we started  filming and they put a vote to the  family and Sydney didn't and Anisha  voted to be in the film so it was very  important to everybody that that they  understand that this material will live  on Deborah is very hard to say no to  that's that's part of the way we got  access that she kept showing that we  kept showing up and saying that we  wanted to make an accurate film and we  were not there to try to do an expose  about the Department of Children and  Family Services we really wanted to  understand the welfare system and I  think people after Patong believed us  and and one of the aspects of that is  that when Mark and I were first talking  about doing in the subject that we felt  that the way the subject is ordinarily  depicted is at the time of a crisis that  the public gets their information when  there's a fatality and something that we  we can all agree is horrific and really  disturbing and that very often the press  or investigative journalists and we  wanted to come from a different point of  view because we felt that when the  public gets their information that the  in that way they turn away and despair  and we wanted to turn that despair into  engagement if at all possible what were  you looking for what were the challenges  in shooting this film what we were  looking for we were looking somebody who  was really good but when we first met  Nick you and we're talking about because  this was very much an observational film  correct and  and were intervening in very dramatic  moments we thought in in very intimate  settings and I had and we were asked  Nick how he did that and Nick said well  I just kind of curl up in a ball and  tried to disappear  he said he puts on a cloak of  invisibility and then he he he squatted  down really to show and he actually did  appear to disappear and the other great  thing about Nick was that we filmed for  90 days and Nick made a commitment and  and stuck with it which is a really big  deal that there's not a different person  coming in to operate and I'm gonna  outside the cloak of invisibility that  you borrowed from Harry Potter what does  that mean in terms of how you shoot what  kind of crew you bring how you blight it  how small can you be and how quickly do  you realize that you have become  invisible to the subjects I think I  learned a valuable lesson one time we  were I was shooting a scene in a hotel  room and it was these people from Comic  Con we were counting up their money and  they were all celebrating because they  had $50,000 and they put them in piles  of thousands and they were like pointed  after 25 and then they counted again and  Kennedy knew this was 350 but there was  only 25 and you could see is like 25  grand missing and the atmosphere in the  room could be changed the producer left  the room went to the bathroom the guy  with the boom pole looked away and I  just corrected hoping they wouldn't see  me  and then they continued with the scene  and then I realized they were accusing  the people behind me they were literally  just touching so then I had to stand up  and turn around and get a shot then I  went back down again and no one said  stop so I was like okay and that's my  method man and part of it is just hiding  behind the camera I just hide your face  but you can see there's a great moment  in the film I think that really a test  and Nick's skill is when the baby has a  seizure and Nick just holds still and  even don't hold still I think I learned  that one just like  like don't do anything just oh it looks  like that baby is not moving it's bad  and then hold still  and it's because then all the people  move into the frame from outside and so  it's like a single basically a single  take cut up a little bit but mark and I  weren't in the room the room was so  small and in order for Nick to get it  all around the room we left because  you'd be in the shot yes so yeah and so  we were on headsets outside the door and  all of a sudden you heard what was going  on the wind it went very silent it went  very silent and everyone just looked at  the baby and I mean you guys were  outside but you knew something serious  was going on because the conversation  just ended and I mean that thing I just  completely stood still and at one point  if you look in that shot there's the  babies the bottles kind of in front his  face and I needed to be two inches  higher but to go two inches higher I  would have to do one of those yoga pose  and I was like I'm not gonna hold that  for very long so I just held it still  again because if I move and draw  attention to myself  it changes the scene and people might  say okay you need to stop filming this  because there's no baby looks in a bad  way okay at what point do you start  figuring out and in consultation with  your with director and your producer  about what are the stories the people  are gonna focus on and how you come up  with a narrative where you feel that you  can stay with somebody for a certain  period of time and come back to somebody  how do you start an assembly on a movie  like this because it seems monumental  yes it was well fortunately when you  have such incredible people to work with  who are so compassionate and kind and  create a space where people open up and  and there's trust they're the kind of  material that we got that was it was  incredible you know it was just so so  powerful so one of the challenges is  bringing it down but I had a - an  assistant Bert Cornish and  another AE youngbleaux long who really  helped to bring the material down so  they would give me long long select  scenes and you kind of just go scene by  scene character by character and putting  each character story together and then  looking for the intersections or the  ways that one story informs another  because I see a film like this with  multiple people in it as like a relay  race someone starts and then it's handed  off to the next person and so forth and  so it's finding those connecting links  and bringing it through and I also  always think in terms of a three-act  structure if you think about how this  film and I mean this truly how its cast  the kinds of stories that you want to  tell within the limitations that you're  facing when you think about Jessica's  story rayon and Chris's story Mary story  Dasani story the mother of the year mrs.  beavers what are you looking for and how  do you figure out why this is a good mix  we made a decision to film entirely in  Los Angeles County for a number of  reasons one because we were all here and  if the stories were taking place across  the country we would not have been able  to capture something like Chris land  seizure or some of the other incidents  in the film so there were economic  interests and also access interests but  we never wanted this movie - we never  wanted to suggest that this movie was  about la we wanted our type of stories  that could take place anywhere across  the country so christen was meant to  represent the earliest point at which a  child can enter the system so that was a  newborn baby with the positive  toxicology and we got phone calls every  day for three months from the hotline  letting us know that another child had  been born with drugs in their system and  if a story wasn't going to work out or  wasn't quite right very sadly we knew  that there would be another phone call  within a very short period of time we  also wanted to show the other end of the  spectrum which was the youth aging out  of the system because not all states  have extended care to 21 and for every  year that a child gets to stay within  the system beyond 18 years of age it  reduces the incidence of homelessness  drug addiction pregnancy early death  really serious consequences and outcomes  for these children Dasani story was put  on our radar by the hilton Foundation  and Barbra Dewey says that line in the  movie about in an intact family if  children acted out that they would  suffer consequences and be punished but  they wouldn't face charges and then we  felt a responsibility to show a good  foster parent we were going down a path  with another woman and we mentioned that  to the Department of Children and Family  Services and they said you think she's  great you should meet mrs. beavers they  were very happy to meet mrs. beavers and  then the final story was Jessica  Chandler who just had experienced so  many different aspects of the system a  probation youth the pregnant teen of  foster youth who wanted to blow up the  entire system and instead represents the  importance of one consistent caring  adult who never gave up on her never  judged her and she as she says she went  from testing at the third grade math  level when she graduated high school to  getting her master's in social work and  now working from within to reform the  system you know those are the kinds of  stories were looking for but in the end  casting is key and for me the best  characters for documentary and for  fictional films as well are people who  are struggling right and we look for  people who want it to be in the film  because being in the film in some ways  help to make sense of their own life how  help to in in some ways it's it's  therapeutic Liz having sympathetic  filmmakers validate their lives and  validate the importance of what they're  going through I think is valuable and  then you find people you know them when  you see them when you meet Jessica you  know that she's a person that so  charismatic so honest close to her  feelings and all the people that we met  in the film were articulate and they  were willing to be open about their  lives and they were and they were  willing to trust us and that those are  the most important things I want to  thank Nick Kate  Deborah and Mark for sharing your time  and bringing us your film

